{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cmp clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-system.txt clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-system.txt && cmp clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-closing.txt clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-closing.txt && printf 'Both cmp comparisons identical\\\\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-schema.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"type\": \"object\",\n \"additionalProperties\": false,\n \"required\": [\n  \"new_theses\",\n  \"evidence\"\n ],\n \"properties\": {\n  \"new_theses\": {\n   \"type\": \"array\",\n   \"description\": \"New thesis proposals, each referenced by at least one evidence link.\",\n   \"items\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\n    \"required\": [\n     \"local_id\",\n     \"description\",\n     \"outcome\",\n     \"period\",\n     \"opens_as\",\n     \"settled_by\",\n     \"closest_open_thesis\",\n     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\n    ],\n    \"properties\": {\n     \"local_id\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"Local reference for this new thesis in the answer; not a database ID.\"\n     },\n     \"description\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"The proposed research question, stated clearly.\"\n     },\n     \"outcome\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"The outcome or event being tracked by this thesis.\"\n     },\n     \"period\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"The period or check date that settles the thesis: the one the item supports, or the one its reason fixes (section 3), with how it was set.\"\n     },\n     \"opens_as\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n       \"product_node\",\n       \"binding_obligation\",\n       \"shared_quantity\",\n       \"policy_stage\"\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"The reason this thesis opens, one of the four in section 3.\"\n     },\n     \"settled_by\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"The public material that will settle the answer on a date (a filing, an official decision, a shipment confirmation, a statistics release).\",\n      \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\"\n     },\n     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Exact ID of the open thesis closest to this one; null when the open list is empty.\"\n     },\n     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"Why an analyst would bet on this question separately from the closest open thesis: how their answers can move apart.\"\n     }\n    }\n   }\n  },\n  \"evidence\": {\n   \"type\": \"array\",\n   \"description\": \"Evidence statements extracted from this item, with their thesis links.\",\n   \"items\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\n    \"required\": [\n     \"local_id\",\n     \"summary\",\n     \"source_passages\",\n     \"original_party\",\n     \"reporting_speaker\",\n     \"tier\",\n     \"statement_date\",\n     \"statement_date_text\",\n     \"evidence_period\",\n     \"links\",\n     \"figures\"\n    ],\n    \"properties\": {\n     \"local_id\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"Local reference for this evidence in the answer; not a database ID.\"\n     },\n     \"summary\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"Standalone faithful summary naming the actor and preserving relevant figures, periods and conditions.\"\n     },\n     \"source_passages\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"description\": \"Contiguous verbatim passages from this source item that jointly support the summary; return each passage separately.\",\n      \"items\": {\n       \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n     },\n     \"original_party\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Party whose action, figure or claim is reported; null if unknown. Use the reporting outlet or author as the original party only for its own estimate, analysis or unnamed-source report.\"\n     },\n     \"reporting_speaker\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Person or outlet making or reporting the statement in this item, when known; null if unknown.\"\n     },\n     \"statement_date\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\",\n        \"pattern\": \"^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Original statement day as YYYY-MM-DD when known; null if the full day is unknown.\"\n     },\n     \"figures\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n       \"type\": \"object\",\n       \"additionalProperties\": false,\n       \"required\": [\n        \"measure\",\n        \"period\",\n        \"fiscal_period\",\n        \"value\",\n        \"kind\",\n        \"party\",\n        \"basis\"\n       ],\n       \"properties\": {\n        \"measure\": {\n         \"type\": \"string\",\n         \"description\": \"The financial measure as the passage states it.\"\n        },\n        \"period\": {\n         \"anyOf\": [\n          {\n           \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          {\n           \"type\": \"null\"\n          }\n         ],\n         \"description\": \"The period as stated; null when none is given.\"\n        },\n        \"fiscal_period\": {\n         \"anyOf\": [\n          {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"pattern\": \"^(FY|CY)[0-9]{4}(-Q[1-4])?$\"\n          },\n          {\n           \"type\": \"null\"\n          }\n         ],\n         \"description\": \"The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself writes the year of this figure's period: FY2027 for the company's fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for the third quarter of that fiscal year, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter. The year may be written anywhere in the item for the same period (\\\"the second quarter of fiscal 2027\\\" once, \\\"that quarter\\\" afterwards). null when no period is given, when the item never writes that year (a quarter or \\\"the full year\\\" with no year; a year that would have to be worked out from another year the item mentions), or when the period is not one year or one quarter (a range, or a deadline such as \\\"by\\\" a year). Never work the year out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item.\"\n        },\n        \"value\": {\n         \"type\": \"string\",\n         \"description\": \"The figure copied exactly from one of this item's passages, with its unit.\"\n        },\n        \"kind\": {\n         \"type\": \"string\",\n         \"enum\": [\n          \"actual\",\n          \"guidance\",\n          \"estimate\"\n         ],\n         \"description\": \"actual: reported, including a balance as of a date; guidance: the company's own outlook; estimate: a named party's or consensus forecast.\"\n        },\n        \"party\": {\n         \"anyOf\": [\n          {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\"\n          },\n          {\n           \"type\": \"null\"\n          }\n         ],\n         \"description\": \"The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement's original_party. For the company's own reported result: the company. For a forecast or outlook: the company only where the item says the company, its management or a named executive gave it (add the executive's name where the item gives the figure as that person's statement); the named person or firm, or \\\"consensus\\\" where the item gives it as analysts' or the market's expectation; the item's author or outlet for its own calculation or estimate. A forecast the item states in its own voice without saying whose it is (\\\"margins are expected to fall\\\", \\\"earnings are projected at\\\") has no stated source: party is null, and its kind is estimate, not guidance. Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item.\"\n        },\n        \"basis\": {\n         \"anyOf\": [\n          {\n           \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          {\n           \"type\": \"null\"\n          }\n         ],\n         \"description\": \"Scope and qualifications as stated (GAAP or non-GAAP, segment or company, conditions); null when none.\"\n        }\n       }\n      },\n      \"description\": \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement. A statement with several useful figures stays one item and lists them all.\"\n     },\n     \"links\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"minItems\": 0,\n      \"description\": \"The existing or proposed theses this evidence informs; empty when it is material but no thesis takes it yet: it is kept in the company's record and linked when a covering thesis opens.\",\n      \"items\": {\n       \"type\": \"object\",\n       \"additionalProperties\": false,\n       \"required\": [\n        \"target_type\",\n        \"target_id\",\n        \"relevance\"\n       ],\n       \"properties\": {\n        \"target_type\": {\n         \"type\": \"string\",\n         \"enum\": [\n          \"existing\",\n          \"new\"\n         ],\n         \"description\": \"Whether the reference identifies an open thesis or a new proposal.\"\n        },\n        \"target_id\": {\n         \"type\": \"string\",\n         \"description\": \"Exact supplied ID when existing, or local_id of a new thesis in this answer.\"\n        },\n        \"relevance\": {\n         \"type\": \"string\",\n         \"description\": \"How this evidence informs the referenced thesis, distinguishing source claims from inference.\"\n        }\n       }\n      }\n     },\n     \"tier\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n       \"fact\",\n       \"party_statement\",\n       \"third_party_view\",\n       \"rumour\"\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Source tier of the statement; an unconfirmed or unnamed-source claim takes precedence as rumour.\"\n     },\n     \"statement_date_text\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Original date or partial date as stated in the item; null when absent.\"\n     },\n     \"evidence_period\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n       {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n       },\n       {\n        \"type\": \"null\"\n       }\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Period the fact, plan or forecast describes, as supported by the item; null when unknown.\"\n     }\n    }\n   }\n  }\n }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/audit-c21-blind.md'","aggregated_output":"**The two new fields fit the approved shape, but their descriptions leave real ambiguity about whose name to write and when a period can be converted.** For example, an analyst’s estimate of the company’s revenue can have the analyst as the figure’s source while the company is the subject of the number. The request does not clearly distinguish those roles.\n\nI compared the parsed JSON: c21 differs from c8 only by the two required figure fields. `cmp` confirmed that both the system text and closing check are byte-for-byte identical. D495 authorizes this test version without changing what is kept, opened or linked; c8 remains the adopted request. The earlier approved shape calls for a useful figure to carry who said it and its period. [D494 and D495](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:5641), [approved shape](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:472)\n\nThe findings below concern wording risks; I did not run the model, so they are not observed changes in its answers.\n\n### 1. Each new description\n\n**`party`**\n\n1. **“Whose figure this one is, as the item states it: the company for its own reported result or its own guidance; the named person, firm or ‘consensus’ for an estimate; the item’s author or outlet for its own calculation or estimate.”**  \n   **Ambiguous.** “Whose figure” can mean the subject of the figure or the person who produced it. For an analyst’s estimate of company revenue, those are different. The sentence intends the source, but the statement-level `original_party` says **“Party whose action, figure or claim is reported”**: a model could reasonably copy that field or regard the two fields as conflicting. They can agree for a company’s result and differ for a mixed statement; the request does not explain that relationship.\n\n   “The company” is defined in the system text as `current_entity`, so the referent is clear, but **the string to return is not**: the model could write the literal “the company,” the entity identifier, or a company name from the article. “As the item states it” also leaves the result unclear when the company is identified by a pronoun or context. The executive-outlook example below adds uncertainty: should guidance voiced by an executive carry the company or the executive?\n\n2. **“In a statement with several figures each takes its own (a reported result beside an executive’s outlook; the company’s figure beside the market’s estimate).”**  \n   **Ambiguous.** “Each takes its own” means an individual value is needed, but does not explain whether an executive’s outlook is the executive’s or the company’s guidance, or what exact name to use for “the market.” It can also be misread as requiring different parties, even where several figures have the same source. The examples are compatible with the existing instruction that several figures stay in one item, but do not explicitly reinforce it.\n\n3. **“null when the item does not say whose it is.”**  \n   **Ambiguous, but provides a necessary fallback.** It is unclear whether a source established by context counts as the item saying whose figure it is. Read literally, it may produce `null` for an implicitly attributed company result; read broadly, it may encourage inference. It does not require dropping the figure.\n\n**`fiscal_period`**\n\n1. **“The same period in fixed form, only when the item establishes it: FY2027 for the company’s fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for its third fiscal quarter, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter.”**  \n   **Ambiguous and sometimes asks for a form the figure cannot supply.** The pattern accepts one fiscal or calendar year, optionally one quarter. It cannot express a multi-year range or a deadline such as “by 2029.” The sentence does not explicitly say to use `null` when the source gives a period that this pattern cannot represent.\n\n   For a company whose fiscal year differs from the calendar year, the examples distinguish `FY` from `CY`, but do not say whether a date range or calendar quarter may be converted into a fiscal quarter using knowledge outside the item. “Only when the item establishes it” points against outside inference, but does not fully settle what counts as establishing it. The example “its third fiscal quarter” also omits the year needed for `FY2027-Q3`.\n\n2. **“A relative period (‘last quarter’, ‘next fiscal year’) takes a fixed form only when the item itself names that quarter or year; null when it does not.”**  \n   **Mostly clear, with a boundary ambiguity.** It gives a null fallback for relative periods, but “names” could mean an explicit label or a period identifiable from the article’s surrounding results context. An article saying “the quarter” while discussing the latest results may therefore get a fixed value if the article names that quarter, but the instruction does not settle cases where the context merely implies it.\n\n3. **“Never work one out from the publication date.”**  \n   **Clear.** It rules out using publication date to calculate a period. It does not resolve whether other article context is enough. This is distinct from `statement_date`, which is the date the original statement was made, and `evidence_period`, which is the period the statement describes.\n\nFor a figure with **no period**, the existing `period` description clearly says `null`; the new description implies but does not explicitly say that `fiscal_period` is also `null`. For a **multi-year range** or **“by 2029,”** selecting a single year would misrepresent the source; the accepted pattern cannot preserve the range or deadline, but the description should state the null outcome.\n\n### 2. Possible effects on what is kept, opened, linked or split\n\nNeither field explicitly changes those decisions. The existing schema says **“A statement with several useful figures stays one item and lists them all,”** and the closing check likewise keeps a repeated measure with its figures as one item. The new “each takes its own” sentence does not override that. Still, it could be misread as requiring one statement per source, particularly for a result alongside an estimate.\n\nThe main risk to figure completeness is the combination of **“Whose figure … as the item states it”** with a required field: a model might omit a figure whose source it cannot name, even though the field permits `null`. That would conflict with the instruction to list all useful figures and the closing check’s direction that a missing amount, date or scale does not by itself drop an item.\n\nA guessed fixed period could also make a later reader or the model treat a figure as belonging to the wrong period. Section 2 explicitly says **“a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked”** and asks the model to preserve period differences. The new wording does not instruct a linking change, but a wrongly inferred period could influence one. I see no sentence in the additions that directly tells the model to open a new thesis.\n\n### 3. Examples\n\nThe `party` examples are generic and do not name a covered company, product or number. They apply across industries, although “executive’s outlook” leaves the attribution ambiguity above.\n\nThe `fiscal_period` examples are also industry-neutral and name no company or product, but **they do contain numbers**: `FY2027`, `FY2027-Q3`, `CY2027` and `CY2027-Q2`. They therefore do not satisfy a literal “no number” requirement. They are format examples rather than company-specific financial figures.\n\n### 4. Smallest wording changes\n\nThese changes clarify the fields without adding a keep, opening or linking rule:\n\n- For `party`, replace “Whose figure this one is” with **“The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement’s `original_party`”**; specify **“use the company name supplied in `current_entity`”** if that input is a name, or otherwise specify the intended company-name source. State that multiple figures may have the same source, and that an executive’s company guidance uses the company if that is the intended attribution. Keep the `null` fallback.\n- For `fiscal_period`, add: **“Use null when no period is given or when the period cannot be represented by one year or quarter in this format, including a range or deadline.”** State that a fixed label must be supported by the item itself, including for relative periods, and that the model must not convert between calendar and fiscal periods using outside knowledge. Retain the prohibition on deriving it from publication date.\n- If examples must contain no numbers at all, describe the accepted format in words rather than using concrete year and quarter examples; the existing schema pattern already defines the permitted characters.\n\nNo files were changed.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-system.txt clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-closing.txt'","aggregated_output":"ROLE\n\nYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one source item for the company in scope (current_entity); \"the company\" below is that\ncompany. The research already tracked for it is listed in open_theses. Your first job is evidence: decide by the rules below which\nstatements in the item are material, then link each one to every thesis it informs: an open thesis that covers it, a new thesis you\npropose, or both. Your second job is new theses: the important research questions this item raises that no open thesis\ncovers. Every new thesis comes with evidence from this item. A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not\nitself a big matter (section 3) is returned with empty links: code keeps it in the company's record without links and links it\nwhen a thesis that covers it opens. Never open a thesis only to give a statement a home.\n\nINPUT\n\n- current_entity and company_tags: the company in scope and the companies the item is tagged with. Code assigns the entity; do not\n  invent one. Evidence can describe another party when it informs research on the company.\n- open_theses: the identity, question, tracked outcome and period of each thesis already tracked. They tell you where evidence belongs\n  and whether an idea is already covered; they are not evidence. You do not see the evidence already stored; a later step compares\n  new evidence with history.\n- source_item: the complete text and its source metadata.\n\n1. EVIDENCE: WHAT TO KEEP\n\n- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: \"same-store sales rose 4.2% in the\n  quarter\"; \"management guided full-year revenue to $12–13 billion\"; \"the board approved a $10 billion buyback\". (2) A customer's,\n  supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand,\n  supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: \"the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year\"; \"a bank\n  chose a rival's system\"; \"a software firm is integrating the company's model into its government systems\"; \"the main supplier said\n  its capacity will double by 2027\"; \"a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe\"; \"the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled\n  to $40 billion\". An action is a step the item reports as news: taken, decided or planned (an order, a contract, a selection, an\n  integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built\n  on the company's products, and the benefits it describes from that use (faster, cheaper, more accurate), describe an arrangement in\n  place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) (\"a hospital chain uses\n  the company's scheduling software and reports shorter waits\"). (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its\n  industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer's or rival's executive or an official makes it: \"the CFO said\n  plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity\"; \"an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10\"; \"an analyst said the\n  bank's loan losses next quarter will come in well above consensus\"; \"a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables\n  by $2 billion\". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or\n  step, including one still in force that the item reports: \"the regulator approved the drug for a second indication\"; \"the\n  government required licences for exports of the product\"; \"the regulator still bars sales of the drug in the country\".\n- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last, including paragraphs about matters other than the headline. The four\n  kinds describe what material statements usually look like; they do not decide what is kept. Take a statement when an analyst\n  following the company would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, and no row\n  of the closing check at the end of this request rejects it; a row's \"it does not cover\" clause sends the statement back to that test\n  and keeps it only where the row says so. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight statements that pass yields eight\n  evidence items, not a selection.\n\n2. LINK EACH STATEMENT TO ITS THESES\n\n- EXISTING: if an open thesis covers the statement, link it to that thesis's exact supplied ID, including contrary facts. Do not\n  return or rename the thesis's description.\n- NEW: if the statement raises a research question that merits separate tracking and no open or newly proposed thesis covers it,\n  propose a thesis (section 3) and link the statement to it.\n- NO THESIS YET: if no open or newly proposed thesis covers a statement you judge material, and the matter is not big enough by\n  its own scale to merit a thesis (section 3), return it with empty links; it is kept without links.\n- CONDITION: a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's\n  or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings\n  path when they bear on that path; a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked. When no open or new\n  thesis covers it, its links stay empty. A figure is useful when it can change what an analyst\n  expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position: a retailer's quarterly\n  same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are; a cash-rich\n  company's cash balance or cash flow usually changes none of these when the item ties it to nothing, while a heavily indebted\n  firm's cash burn changes its credit risk. The share price, a target price and other companies' figures are never conditions.\n- Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform; use the narrowest\n  relevant thesis only when choosing between theses that cover the same thing.\n- Match by the underlying question or outcome, subject and scope, and time when relevant, not by word overlap. A different wording,\n  source, forecast or direction alone does not make a new thesis. Related questions can be separate when their outcomes matter\n  independently.\n- Do not exclude evidence solely because it concerns a related period, population or product variant. Assess whether it can inform\n  the open question, preserving the difference in the record. Do not present a forecast for a later year as a forecast for the\n  thesis's year. If another open thesis covers the evidence more directly, consider that thesis as well; do not assume different\n  periods are automatically the same thesis or automatically unrelated.\n\nExamples of linking evidence to research:\n\n- Supply chain: an open thesis asks whether a manufacturer's production is constrained. A named supplier announces a capacity\n  expansion for a component the manufacturer uses. Link the announcement as a potential supply signal; it does not establish\n  allocations or deliveries.\n- Geopolitics: an open thesis asks whether an exporter can sell in a specified market. The relevant government proposes a licensing\n  restriction on that product. Link the proposal as evidence about market access; a proposal is not an enacted restriction.\n- Macro: an open thesis asks whether a company's borrowing costs will rise. The item reports that its floating-rate debt is tied to a\n  benchmark that has risen. Link the benchmark movement and the stated exposure; do not calculate the expense change unless the\n  source supports it.\n- Competition: an open thesis asks whether a company can retain customers in a product market. A named competitor cuts the price of a\n  competing product. Link the price cut as competitive evidence; do not assert lost customers or share without support.\n\n3. WHEN TO PROPOSE A NEW THESIS\n\nA thesis is a question a senior analyst would hold a card for: its answer could change a position because it could change the\ncompany's revenue, profit, cash or credit risk, or competitive position in a way you can name; public material will settle it on a\ndate; and no open thesis already asks it. A thesis asks one quantity for one period, or one event. It opens for one of four reasons, a closed set; a matter that fits none of them is\nevidence, kept and linked to the theses it informs or left unlinked, never a thesis:\n- product_node: when the generation of the main product that carries the company's value reaches the step that brings revenue,\n  usually first shipments; announcements and samples are its evidence, and only milestones an analyst bets on separately (a drug's\n  trial result and its approval) are separate theses (\"will the carmaker's new platform start deliveries in 2026?\";\n  \"will the drugmaker's next-generation inhaler, the product line that carries its value, reach pharmacies by mid-2027?\").\n- binding_obligation: an obligation the company takes on in its own name, large enough to change its revenue, profit, cash or credit\n  risk: guaranteeing, backing or giving credit support for another party's debt, lease, revenue or capacity, lending to it,\n  committing to buy capacity or services, or a sized stake or acquisition not yet completed. One already undertaken is tracked by its\n  amount at the next annual report; one not yet signed by whether it is signed by a check date within 18 months of the item (\"how much will the\n  utility have guaranteed for its project companies' loans at its next annual report?\"; \"will the retailer sign a guarantee for its\n  franchisees' leases, large enough to change its credit risk, by the check date?\"). Its own borrowing or leases and a completed\n  or unsized investment are not obligations.\n- shared_quantity: a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year:\n  several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate (\"the largest container lines' combined capacity\n  added in 2026\"; \"next year's average contract price of a raw material\"). Another population or wording of the same aggregate is\n  evidence for it.\n- policy_stage: the step a policy or regulatory process reaches, naming who decides (\"will the regulator approve the drug for a second\n  indication?\"; \"will the government require licences for the product's exports by a date?\").\n\n- Open only matters an analyst would bet on separately; small matters open no thesis and their statements are kept as evidence. A\n  customer's deployment plan is evidence for the company's demand and product theses, unless it changes the company's own cash or\n  credit risk, which is judged as an obligation.\n- Give the date the item supports. When it gives none, use the date the reason fixes and say how it was set: an obligation already\n  undertaken, its next annual report; an unsigned obligation, a check date within 18 months of the item; a pending regulatory\n  decision, the current fiscal year-end. A shared quantity takes its release date and a product event the date its outcome can be\n  checked, only when the item supports one. A question no date can settle is rewritten or not opened.\n- Do not open a thesis about: an executive appointment or organisational change; the company's own borrowing or leases as an\n  obligation; a buyback authorisation or dividend declaration; a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period,\n  on its own; an undated moat or strategy; a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a\n  product already on sale; another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks; an annual quantity after next year (an\n  obligation excepted); shareholder votes, bylaws or routine litigation procedure. Their statements are evidence for the theses they\n  inform, or left unlinked.\n- Before proposing a new thesis, name its reason (opens_as), the public material that will settle it (settled_by), and the closest\n  open thesis with why an analyst would bet on the two separately because their answers can move apart; with no open thesis, say so.\n  If the two cannot move apart, link the evidence to every open thesis it informs instead.\n\nCounterexamples:\n\n- \"The company's outlook is interesting\" or \"the industry may change\" is too vague: it names no outcome later evidence can inform.\n- If an open thesis tracks sales growth, a revised sales forecast is evidence for it; do not open a second thesis to repeat it.\n- A customer expanding capacity informs demand; it is not a thesis of its own, and not a claim that the supplier has received orders.\n- \"Is the stock attractively valued?\" is an investment conclusion, not a research question about the business.\n\n4. HOW TO WRITE EVIDENCE\n\n- One evidence item records one statement, and that statement is what you judge: a figure, date or comparison in the neighbouring\n  words does not make another claim in the same passage new or material. A statement told in several sentences of the item, with its\n  terms, figures and conditions, is one item.\n- Write a standalone summary: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring\n  sentence gives it. Preserve conditions, uncertainty and attribution. Return every passage the summary rests on, each copied\n  verbatim and contiguously from the item, without translation; do not join distant passages with an ellipsis.\n- Name the actor in the summary and identify the original party whose action, figure or claim it is. Give the reporting speaker when\n  available. The item's publisher and publication date come from its source metadata.\n- Use only this item's information. Distinguish forecasts, allegations and unconfirmed reports from confirmed outcomes. Report plans\n  as plans; do not infer committed funding, orders, deliveries or revenue.\n- For each item, return its evidence tier, the original statement date and the period the statement describes. These are separate\n  pieces of information.\n\nEVIDENCE TIER\n\nClassify the statement by what is confirmed, not how important it is:\n\n- rumour: the item attributes the claim to unnamed sources or calls it unconfirmed.\n- fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction\n  reported by an article.\n- party_statement: a company's guidance, management statement or official's remarks. A forecast is a statement, not a completed\n  outcome.\n- third_party_view: an analyst's, journalist's or expert's opinion or estimate.\n\nApply the first matching category in that order. An unnamed-source or unconfirmed claim remains a rumour even if it describes an\naction or number. If none matches and the claim is unattributed, use rumour. The tier is not an importance score.\n\nEVIDENCE TIME\n\n- statement_date: the original statement's full day in YYYY-MM-DD when known; otherwise null. Do not use the article date as the\n  original date unless the statement was made in the originating party's own release, filing, call or analysis. Do not assign that\n  date to an older statement quoted there.\n- statement_date_text: the original date or partial date as stated, if present; otherwise null. Preserve a month or relative date\n  without inventing a day. A forecast horizon or an occasion is not a date.\n- evidence_period: the period the reported fact, plan or forecast covers, as supported by the item; otherwise null. This is not the\n  statement date.\n\nReturn the theses and evidence in the supplied response schema, after the closing check at the end of this request. Return empty\nlists when nothing passes.\nBefore you return the answer, check it once. Read each evidence item back as the analyst following the company and keep it when you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, about the company or its customers, suppliers, competitors, regulators or the economy. Ask first whether it is new: repeated common knowledge, a long-standing situation retold and an unargued view are noise. Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it or another outlet reported it before: a later step compares each statement with the evidence already stored and records a repeat there. Guidance, a forecast, a plan or a decision still in force keeps its place even when retold, including a change to a plan, design or schedule that another outlet reported days or weeks before. Judge the action or information itself, not its verb tense, and do not take a statement as old because its date is missing. Fitting one of the four kinds does not keep an item. A missing amount, date or scale never drops an item on its own: a forecast, a rumour, a counter-example or a distant or conditional statement is judged like any other.\nThese are not kept, even when a named party states them. What a row says it does not cover goes back to the test above, unless the row says it is kept:\n- background the analyst already knew, such as the company's history (\"the company was founded in 1998\"); an arrangement the item only describes as in place, even when no date is given: customers or partners that use, host, run or have built on the company's products, and the benefits they describe from that use (\"two airlines fly the planemaker's jets\"; \"three insurers run claims on the company's software and report faster payouts\"); a partnership, contract, plant or investment the item itself dates to an earlier month or year with no new step (\"earlier this year the company partnered with the glassmaker on three plants\"; \"in May it signed a $3 billion cloud contract\"); it does not cover a step the item reports as news, taken, decided, planned or under way (an order, a selection, an integration, an expansion, a deployment), even when it gives no date (\"the retailer's largest supplier is opening its own stores, people familiar said this week\"; \"the two largest cement makers are adding kilns to meet demand\"), nor a customer's, supplier's or partner's newly disclosed revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amount; a deal the item mentions as already existing is an arrangement in place even with its amount (\"a rival airline already leases 40 of the same jets\");\n- a typical claim that is common knowledge, without a figure and a specific account: that a firm leads or competes, that demand is strong or supply tight, a general risk (\"if demand slows it would hurt\"); a number does not lift a familiar position (\"the rival grocer has led this market for years and still holds 40%\"); it does not cover a dated forecast of a shortage, a price or demand (\"the steelmaker expects the shortage to peak next year and last until 2028\"), a competitor's newly reported share, volume or price in the company's market (\"the local rival now holds about half of the regional market, worth about $5 billion\"), nor a reported movement of a named price, rental rate, lead time or order book, even without a figure (\"rental rates for used trucks have been climbing this quarter\"); a general statement that demand stays strong or a product stays popular is not one;\n- a product, game, driver or benchmark specification with no business scale (\"the vacuum runs 60 minutes on a charge\"; \"a benchmark score\"), a retail price detail (\"a retailer marks one model down by $50\"), and a comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes (\"the maker says its drug works twice as fast as older ones\"; \"a reviewer's test\"); it does not cover a competitor's comparison against the company's product, a partner's or customer's adoption or integration (also when a comparison shares its sentence), a reported launch, shipment, delay or cancellation of any of the company's products or a change to a product plan or configuration, including a reported sign that a planned product is going ahead (\"the long-range version of the jet is likely delayed or limited to a few deliveries\"; \"a parts supplier's catalogue lists the unannounced model\"), nor the company's decision to change the price it charges; uncertainty or a smaller product line does not make it a specification;\n- an unargued view or a quote that only characterises (\"the stock is a hold\"; \"a pivotal moment\"; \"an industry-leading platform\"), and a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no new fact or calculation (\"the recall shows quality risks are rising\"); it does not cover a reading that adds a new fact or calculation, nor the company's own statement that its plan, schedule or guidance still stands (\"the carmaker said the launch date has not moved\"), which is judged as the plan itself would be; a target price or valuation of the company is not kept, even with its assumptions and derivation, while the estimates it rests on are judged on their own (an analysts' earnings forecast is kept);\n- a political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision (\"diversifying suppliers will not reduce dependence\"); it does not cover a policy or regulatory step, nor a decision still in force that the item reports;\n- a share-price move, an investor's position or trade, or the market value (\"shares fell 13% this month\"; \"he is short the stock\"); it does not cover the news behind the move;\n- another company's matter that touches none of the company's markets (its valuation, share price, governance or a deal outside them), or that sits next to the company only by sharing an article, an event or an ecosystem; you supply the connection as the analyst and the item need not state it: a customer's spending, orders or backlog bears on demand, a supplier's capacity, output, prices or revenue on supply and input costs, a rival's products, prices or share in the company's markets on competition (\"a large customer's order backlog doubled\"; \"the main supplier's quarterly revenue jumped on demand for the part\");\n- the same fact said again in other words within this item: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording (\"a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years\"; \"cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total\"); a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own evidence item, and a fact another outlet reported before is not a repeat within this item;\n- company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.\nA report and another party's denial or confirmation of it are two items, each kept only if it passes the test above; an estimate stays apart from any valuation drawn from it, and the valuation is not kept; one party's statement keeps its own planned or rumoured details.\nReturn only the evidence items you keep.\nThen check the theses and links. A new thesis that asks the same underlying question, about the same subject and scope, as an open thesis or another new thesis is not proposed: link its evidence to that thesis. A new thesis needs at least one kept evidence item, and a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed. Each kept evidence item links to every thesis it informs and to no thesis its own relevance says it does not inform.\nFor each new thesis, check its opens_as, settled_by, closest_open_thesis and why_a_separate_bet: drop the thesis if it fits none of the four reasons in section 3, if no public material will settle it on a date, if an open thesis exists and its answer cannot move apart from it, or if it falls under a 'do not open' item in section 3; then link its evidence to every open thesis it informs, or leave it without links.\nAn item whose only home would be a new thesis that is not a big matter is returned with empty links and kept without links; an item an open thesis covers is linked there, not left without links. Then read back each statement you return with empty links that states the company's own figures: return it only if a figure can change what an analyst expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position; for example, a cash-rich company's balance or cash flow that the item ties to nothing usually cannot; return only what passes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cmp clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-system.txt clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-system.txt && cmp clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-closing.txt clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-closing.txt && printf 'Both cmp comparisons identical\\\\n'\"","aggregated_output":"Both cmp comparisons identical\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path\np=Path(\\\"clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms\\\")\na={n:json.loads((p/f\\\"{n}-schema.json\\\").read_text()) for n in (\\\"c8\\\",\\\"c21\\\",\\\"c22\\\")}\ndef diff(x,y,path=\\\"\"'$\"):\n if type(x)!=type(y): print(path,repr(x),repr(y)); return\n if isinstance(x,dict):\n  for k in x.keys()|y.keys():\n   if k not in x or k not in y: print(path+\".\"+k,\"ADDED/REMOVED\",repr(x.get(k)),repr(y.get(k)))\n   else: diff(x[k],y[k],path+\".\"+k)\n elif isinstance(x,list):\n  if x!=y: print(path,repr(x),repr(y))\n elif x!=y: print(path,repr(x),repr(y))\nfor x,y in ((\"c8\",\"c21\"),(\"c21\",\"c22\"),(\"c8\",\"c22\")):\n print(\"COMPARE\",x,y); diff(a[x],a[y])'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path\np=Path(\\\"clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms\\\")\na={n:json.loads((p/f\\\"{n}-schema.json\\\").read_text()) for n in (\\\"c8\\\",\\\"c21\\\",\\\"c22\\\")}\ndef diff(x,y,path=\\\"\"'$\"):\n if type(x)!=type(y): print(path,repr(x),repr(y)); return\n if isinstance(x,dict):\n  for k in x.keys()|y.keys():\n   if k not in x or k not in y: print(path+\".\"+k,\"ADDED/REMOVED\",repr(x.get(k)),repr(y.get(k)))\n   else: diff(x[k],y[k],path+\".\"+k)\n elif isinstance(x,list):\n  if x!=y: print(path,repr(x),repr(y))\n elif x!=y: print(path,repr(x),repr(y))\nfor x,y in ((\"c8\",\"c21\"),(\"c21\",\"c22\"),(\"c8\",\"c22\")):\n print(\"COMPARE\",x,y); diff(a[x],a[y])'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"python3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-v92gFrP4' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-14 20:18:15.726 xcodebuild[68615:49268302]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-14 20:18:15.837 xcodebuild[68615:49268298] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-bTCt9ryC' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-14 20:18:16.150 xcodebuild[68618:49268337]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-14 20:18:16.260 xcodebuild[68618:49268336] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\nCOMPARE c8 c21\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.required ['measure', 'period', 'value', 'kind', 'basis'] ['measure', 'period', 'fiscal_period', 'value', 'kind', 'party', 'basis']\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.properties.fiscal_period ADDED/REMOVED None {'anyOf': [{'type': 'string', 'pattern': '^(FY|CY)[0-9]{4}(-Q[1-4])?$'}, {'type': 'null'}], 'description': 'The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself establishes it: FY2027 for the company\\'s fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for the third quarter of that fiscal year, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter. A relative period (\"last quarter\", \"next fiscal year\") takes a fixed form only when the item itself names that quarter or year. null when no period is given, when the item does not establish the year or quarter, or when the period is not one year or one quarter (a range, or a deadline such as \"by\" a year). Never work one out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item, and do not convert a calendar period into a fiscal one.'}\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.properties.party ADDED/REMOVED None {'anyOf': [{'type': 'string', 'pattern': '\\\\S'}, {'type': 'null'}], 'description': 'The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement\\'s original_party. For the company\\'s own reported result or its own guidance: the company (add an executive\\'s name only where the item gives that figure as the executive\\'s statement). For an estimate: the named person or firm, or \"consensus\" where the item gives it as analysts\\' or the market\\'s expectation. For the item\\'s own calculation or estimate: its author or outlet. Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item. null when the item does not say whose figure it is.'}\nCOMPARE c21 c22\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.properties.fiscal_period.description 'The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself establishes it: FY2027 for the company\\'s fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for the third quarter of that fiscal year, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter. A relative period (\"last quarter\", \"next fiscal year\") takes a fixed form only when the item itself names that quarter or year. null when no period is given, when the item does not establish the year or quarter, or when the period is not one year or one quarter (a range, or a deadline such as \"by\" a year). Never work one out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item, and do not convert a calendar period into a fiscal one.' 'The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself writes the year of this figure\\'s period: FY2027 for the company\\'s fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for the third quarter of that fiscal year, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter. The year may be written anywhere in the item for the same period (\"the second quarter of fiscal 2027\" once, \"that quarter\" afterwards). null when no period is given, when the item never writes that year (a quarter or \"the full year\" with no year; a year that would have to be worked out from another year the item mentions), or when the period is not one year or one quarter (a range, or a deadline such as \"by\" a year). Never work the year out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item.'\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.properties.party.description 'The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement\\'s original_party. For the company\\'s own reported result or its own guidance: the company (add an executive\\'s name only where the item gives that figure as the executive\\'s statement). For an estimate: the named person or firm, or \"consensus\" where the item gives it as analysts\\' or the market\\'s expectation. For the item\\'s own calculation or estimate: its author or outlet. Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item. null when the item does not say whose figure it is.' 'The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement\\'s original_party. For the company\\'s own reported result: the company. For a forecast or outlook: the company only where the item says the company, its management or a named executive gave it (add the executive\\'s name where the item gives the figure as that person\\'s statement); the named person or firm, or \"consensus\" where the item gives it as analysts\\' or the market\\'s expectation; the item\\'s author or outlet for its own calculation or estimate. A forecast the item states in its own voice without saying whose it is (\"margins are expected to fall\", \"earnings are projected at\") has no stated source: party is null, and its kind is estimate, not guidance. Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item.'\nCOMPARE c8 c22\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.required ['measure', 'period', 'value', 'kind', 'basis'] ['measure', 'period', 'fiscal_period', 'value', 'kind', 'party', 'basis']\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.properties.fiscal_period ADDED/REMOVED None {'anyOf': [{'type': 'string', 'pattern': '^(FY|CY)[0-9]{4}(-Q[1-4])?$'}, {'type': 'null'}], 'description': 'The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself writes the year of this figure\\'s period: FY2027 for the company\\'s fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for the third quarter of that fiscal year, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter. The year may be written anywhere in the item for the same period (\"the second quarter of fiscal 2027\" once, \"that quarter\" afterwards). null when no period is given, when the item never writes that year (a quarter or \"the full year\" with no year; a year that would have to be worked out from another year the item mentions), or when the period is not one year or one quarter (a range, or a deadline such as \"by\" a year). Never work the year out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item.'}\n$.properties.evidence.items.properties.figures.items.properties.party ADDED/REMOVED None {'anyOf': [{'type': 'string', 'pattern': '\\\\S'}, {'type': 'null'}], 'description': 'The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement\\'s original_party. For the company\\'s own reported result: the company. For a forecast or outlook: the company only where the item says the company, its management or a named executive gave it (add the executive\\'s name where the item gives the figure as that person\\'s statement); the named person or firm, or \"consensus\" where the item gives it as analysts\\' or the market\\'s expectation; the item\\'s author or outlet for its own calculation or estimate. A forecast the item states in its own voice without saying whose it is (\"margins are expected to fall\", \"earnings are projected at\") has no stated source: party is null, and its kind is estimate, not guidance. Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item.'}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-closing.txt'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-closing.txt'","aggregated_output":"Before you return the answer, check it once. Read each evidence item back as the analyst following the company and keep it when you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, about the company or its customers, suppliers, competitors, regulators or the economy. Ask first whether it is new: repeated common knowledge, a long-standing situation retold and an unargued view are noise. Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it or another outlet reported it before: a later step compares each statement with the evidence already stored and records a repeat there. Guidance, a forecast, a plan or a decision still in force keeps its place even when retold, including a change to a plan, design or schedule that another outlet reported days or weeks before. Judge the action or information itself, not its verb tense, and do not take a statement as old because its date is missing. Fitting one of the four kinds does not keep an item. A missing amount, date or scale never drops an item on its own: a forecast, a rumour, a counter-example or a distant or conditional statement is judged like any other.\nThese are not kept, even when a named party states them. What a row says it does not cover goes back to the test above, unless the row says it is kept:\n- background the analyst already knew, such as the company's history (\"the company was founded in 1998\"); an arrangement the item only describes as in place, even when no date is given: customers or partners that use, host, run or have built on the company's products, and the benefits they describe from that use (\"two airlines fly the planemaker's jets\"; \"three insurers run claims on the company's software and report faster payouts\"); a partnership, contract, plant or investment the item itself dates to an earlier month or year with no new step (\"earlier this year the company partnered with the glassmaker on three plants\"; \"in May it signed a $3 billion cloud contract\"); it does not cover a step the item reports as news, taken, decided, planned or under way (an order, a selection, an integration, an expansion, a deployment), even when it gives no date (\"the retailer's largest supplier is opening its own stores, people familiar said this week\"; \"the two largest cement makers are adding kilns to meet demand\"), nor a customer's, supplier's or partner's newly disclosed revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amount; a deal the item mentions as already existing is an arrangement in place even with its amount (\"a rival airline already leases 40 of the same jets\");\n- a typical claim that is common knowledge, without a figure and a specific account: that a firm leads or competes, that demand is strong or supply tight, a general risk (\"if demand slows it would hurt\"); a number does not lift a familiar position (\"the rival grocer has led this market for years and still holds 40%\"); it does not cover a dated forecast of a shortage, a price or demand (\"the steelmaker expects the shortage to peak next year and last until 2028\"), a competitor's newly reported share, volume or price in the company's market (\"the local rival now holds about half of the regional market, worth about $5 billion\"), nor a reported movement of a named price, rental rate, lead time or order book, even without a figure (\"rental rates for used trucks have been climbing this quarter\"); a general statement that demand stays strong or a product stays popular is not one;\n- a product, game, driver or benchmark specification with no business scale (\"the vacuum runs 60 minutes on a charge\"; \"a benchmark score\"), a retail price detail (\"a retailer marks one model down by $50\"), and a comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes (\"the maker says its drug works twice as fast as older ones\"; \"a reviewer's test\"); it does not cover a competitor's comparison against the company's product, a partner's or customer's adoption or integration (also when a comparison shares its sentence), a reported launch, shipment, delay or cancellation of any of the company's products or a change to a product plan or configuration, including a reported sign that a planned product is going ahead (\"the long-range version of the jet is likely delayed or limited to a few deliveries\"; \"a parts supplier's catalogue lists the unannounced model\"), nor the company's decision to change the price it charges; uncertainty or a smaller product line does not make it a specification;\n- an unargued view or a quote that only characterises (\"the stock is a hold\"; \"a pivotal moment\"; \"an industry-leading platform\"), and a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no new fact or calculation (\"the recall shows quality risks are rising\"); it does not cover a reading that adds a new fact or calculation, nor the company's own statement that its plan, schedule or guidance still stands (\"the carmaker said the launch date has not moved\"), which is judged as the plan itself would be; a target price or valuation of the company is not kept, even with its assumptions and derivation, while the estimates it rests on are judged on their own (an analysts' earnings forecast is kept);\n- a political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision (\"diversifying suppliers will not reduce dependence\"); it does not cover a policy or regulatory step, nor a decision still in force that the item reports;\n- a share-price move, an investor's position or trade, or the market value (\"shares fell 13% this month\"; \"he is short the stock\"); it does not cover the news behind the move;\n- another company's matter that touches none of the company's markets (its valuation, share price, governance or a deal outside them), or that sits next to the company only by sharing an article, an event or an ecosystem; you supply the connection as the analyst and the item need not state it: a customer's spending, orders or backlog bears on demand, a supplier's capacity, output, prices or revenue on supply and input costs, a rival's products, prices or share in the company's markets on competition (\"a large customer's order backlog doubled\"; \"the main supplier's quarterly revenue jumped on demand for the part\");\n- the same fact said again in other words within this item: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording (\"a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years\"; \"cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total\"); a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own evidence item, and a fact another outlet reported before is not a repeat within this item;\n- company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.\nA report and another party's denial or confirmation of it are two items, each kept only if it passes the test above; an estimate stays apart from any valuation drawn from it, and the valuation is not kept; one party's statement keeps its own planned or rumoured details.\nReturn only the evidence items you keep.\nThen check the theses and links. A new thesis that asks the same underlying question, about the same subject and scope, as an open thesis or another new thesis is not proposed: link its evidence to that thesis. A new thesis needs at least one kept evidence item, and a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed. Each kept evidence item links to every thesis it informs and to no thesis its own relevance says it does not inform.\nFor each new thesis, check its opens_as, settled_by, closest_open_thesis and why_a_separate_bet: drop the thesis if it fits none of the four reasons in section 3, if no public material will settle it on a date, if an open thesis exists and its answer cannot move apart from it, or if it falls under a 'do not open' item in section 3; then link its evidence to every open thesis it informs, or leave it without links.\nAn item whose only home would be a new thesis that is not a big matter is returned with empty links and kept without links; an item an open thesis covers is linked there, not left without links. Then read back each statement you return with empty links that states the company's own figures: return it only if a figure can change what an analyst expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position; for example, a cash-rich company's balance or cash flow that the item ties to nothing usually cannot; return only what passes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'figure|forecast|period|date|party|speaker|source|guidance|estimate' clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-system.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'figure|forecast|period|date|party|speaker|source|guidance|estimate' clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-system.txt\"","aggregated_output":"3:You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one source item for the company in scope (current_entity); \"the company\" below is that\n14:  invent one. Evidence can describe another party when it informs research on the company.\n15:- open_theses: the identity, question, tracked outcome and period of each thesis already tracked. They tell you where evidence belongs\n18:- source_item: the complete text and its source metadata.\n22:- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: \"same-store sales rose 4.2% in the\n24:  supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand,\n29:  integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built\n31:  place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) (\"a hospital chain uses\n32:  the company's scheduling software and reports shorter waits\"). (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its\n33:  industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer's or rival's executive or an official makes it: \"the CFO said\n34:  plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity\"; \"an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10\"; \"an analyst said the\n54:- CONDITION: a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's\n55:  or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings\n56:  path when they bear on that path; a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked. When no open or new\n57:  thesis covers it, its links stay empty. A figure is useful when it can change what an analyst\n59:  same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are; a cash-rich\n61:  firm's cash burn changes its credit risk. The share price, a target price and other companies' figures are never conditions.\n65:  source, forecast or direction alone does not make a new thesis. Related questions can be separate when their outcomes matter\n67:- Do not exclude evidence solely because it concerns a related period, population or product variant. Assess whether it can inform\n68:  the open question, preserving the difference in the record. Do not present a forecast for a later year as a forecast for the\n70:  periods are automatically the same thesis or automatically unrelated.\n81:  source supports it.\n89:date; and no open thesis already asks it. A thesis asks one quantity for one period, or one event. It opens for one of four reasons, a closed set; a matter that fits none of them is\n96:  risk: guaranteeing, backing or giving credit support for another party's debt, lease, revenue or capacity, lending to it,\n98:  amount at the next annual report; one not yet signed by whether it is signed by a check date within 18 months of the item (\"how much will the\n100:  franchisees' leases, large enough to change its credit risk, by the check date?\"). Its own borrowing or leases and a completed\n107:  indication?\"; \"will the government require licences for the product's exports by a date?\").\n112:- Give the date the item supports. When it gives none, use the date the reason fixes and say how it was set: an obligation already\n113:  undertaken, its next annual report; an unsigned obligation, a check date within 18 months of the item; a pending regulatory\n114:  decision, the current fiscal year-end. A shared quantity takes its release date and a product event the date its outcome can be\n115:  checked, only when the item supports one. A question no date can settle is rewritten or not opened.\n117:  obligation; a buyback authorisation or dividend declaration; a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period,\n118:  on its own; an undated moat or strategy; a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a\n119:  product already on sale; another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks; an annual quantity after next year (an\n129:- If an open thesis tracks sales growth, a revised sales forecast is evidence for it; do not open a second thesis to repeat it.\n135:- One evidence item records one statement, and that statement is what you judge: a figure, date or comparison in the neighbouring\n137:  terms, figures and conditions, is one item.\n138:- Write a standalone summary: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring\n141:- Name the actor in the summary and identify the original party whose action, figure or claim it is. Give the reporting speaker when\n142:  available. The item's publisher and publication date come from its source metadata.\n143:- Use only this item's information. Distinguish forecasts, allegations and unconfirmed reports from confirmed outcomes. Report plans\n145:- For each item, return its evidence tier, the original statement date and the period the statement describes. These are separate\n152:- rumour: the item attributes the claim to unnamed sources or calls it unconfirmed.\n153:- fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction\n155:- party_statement: a company's guidance, management statement or official's remarks. A forecast is a statement, not a completed\n157:- third_party_view: an analyst's, journalist's or expert's opinion or estimate.\n159:Apply the first matching category in that order. An unnamed-source or unconfirmed claim remains a rumour even if it describes an\n164:- statement_date: the original statement's full day in YYYY-MM-DD when known; otherwise null. Do not use the article date as the\n165:  original date unless the statement was made in the originating party's own release, filing, call or analysis. Do not assign that\n166:  date to an older statement quoted there.\n167:- statement_date_text: the original date or partial date as stated, if present; otherwise null. Preserve a month or relative date\n168:  without inventing a day. A forecast horizon or an occasion is not a date.\n169:- evidence_period: the period the reported fact, plan or forecast covers, as supported by the item; otherwise null. This is not the\n170:  statement date.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-schema.json | rg 'description.*(source of this figure|same period in fixed|actual: reported|company.s own useful)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-schema.json | rg 'description.*(source of this figure|same period in fixed|actual: reported|company.s own useful)'\"","aggregated_output":"   192\t         \"description\": \"The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself writes the year of this figure's period: FY2027 for the company's fiscal year 2027, FY2027-Q3 for the third quarter of that fiscal year, CY2027 or CY2027-Q2 for a calendar year or quarter. The year may be written anywhere in the item for the same period (\\\"the second quarter of fiscal 2027\\\" once, \\\"that quarter\\\" afterwards). null when no period is given, when the item never writes that year (a quarter or \\\"the full year\\\" with no year; a year that would have to be worked out from another year the item mentions), or when the period is not one year or one quarter (a range, or a deadline such as \\\"by\\\" a year). Never work the year out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item.\"\n   205\t         \"description\": \"actual: reported, including a balance as of a date; guidance: the company's own outlook; estimate: a named party's or consensus forecast.\"\n   217\t         \"description\": \"The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement's original_party. For the company's own reported result: the company. For a forecast or outlook: the company only where the item says the company, its management or a named executive gave it (add the executive's name where the item gives the figure as that person's statement); the named person or firm, or \\\"consensus\\\" where the item gives it as analysts' or the market's expectation; the item's author or outlet for its own calculation or estimate. A forecast the item states in its own voice without saying whose it is (\\\"margins are expected to fall\\\", \\\"earnings are projected at\\\") has no stated source: party is null, and its kind is estimate, not guidance. Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item.\"\n   232\t      \"description\": \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement. A statement with several useful figures stays one item and lists them all.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"**The rewording fixes several c21 ambiguities, but it leaves two material conflicts:** an unattributed forecast is told to use `estimate` even though `kind` defines an estimate as having a named or consensus source, and a forecast whose source is stated elsewhere in the article can be read as source-less.\n\nFor example, an article may say “management guided revenue to $100 billion” and later say “the guide implies 20% growth.” The second figure has a stated source in context, but “the item states in its own voice without saying whose it is” does not clearly say whether that context counts.\n\nThe parsed comparison confirms that c21 added the two required figure fields to c8, and **c22 changes only their descriptions** relative to c21. Both `cmp` checks succeeded: c22’s system text and closing check are byte-for-byte identical to c8’s. This is a reading audit; I did not send requests or measure model behavior. The wording is in [c22-schema.json](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c22-schema.json:192).\n\n### 1. Sentence-by-sentence reading\n\n**`party`**\n\n1. **“The source of this figure, which may differ from the statement’s original_party.” — Clear.** It distinguishes the figure’s source from the statement-level field. The exact name format remains unspecified, but that does not prevent identifying the source.\n\n2. **“For the company’s own reported result: the company.” — Clear for an identified company result.** A reported result needs no named spokesperson. The system defines “the company” as `current_entity`. It remains possible for the model to mistake an analyst’s estimate *about* the company for the company’s own result, but the wording itself separates a reported result from a forecast.\n\n3. **“For a forecast or outlook: the company only where the item says the company, its management or a named executive gave it …; the named person or firm, or ‘consensus’ …; the item’s author or outlet for its own calculation or estimate.” — Mostly clear, with a context ambiguity.** Management’s or the company’s stated forecast takes the company; an executive’s name is added when the figure is given as that person’s statement. An analyst’s estimate takes the named analyst or firm, or `consensus` when the article gives it as the market’s or analysts’ expectation. An identifiable author’s own calculation takes the author or outlet.\n\n   The unresolved point is **how attribution elsewhere in the article carries to a later figure**. “Where the item says … gave it” permits context, but does not say how clearly a later reference must connect to that earlier attribution. Also, “add the executive’s name” does not specify a string format; that is a formatting ambiguity, not an attribution conflict.\n\n4. **“A forecast the item states in its own voice without saying whose it is … has no stated source: party is null, and its kind is estimate, not guidance.” — Ambiguous in context and conflicts with `kind`.** For an article-voice forecast with no source stated anywhere, the intended `party` is clearly `null`. But “states in its own voice” can also describe a paraphrase of management guidance attributed elsewhere; it does not say that the *whole article* lacks an attribution.\n\n   The `kind` instruction conflicts with its own description: **“estimate: a named party’s or consensus forecast.”** An unattributed forecast is neither necessarily named nor consensus. The instruction also rules out `guidance` for it, while `kind` defines guidance as **“the company’s own outlook”** without saying that the company must be explicitly identified as its source. The result is an inconsistent classification instruction, even though the new sentence’s intended outcome is apparent.\n\n5. **“Figures of one statement may share a source or have different ones; the statement stays one item.” — Clear.** It agrees with the figure-array description: **“A statement with several useful figures stays one item and lists them all.”**\n\n**`fiscal_period`**\n\n1. **“The same period in fixed form, only when the item itself writes the year of this figure’s period …” — Mostly clear, but incomplete about the quarter and fiscal/calendar marker.** It requires the relevant year to be written and gives examples of fiscal and calendar forms. For a quarter, it does not explicitly say that the quarter number must also be stated or explicitly connected to the figure. Nor does it say whether a bare year is enough to choose `FY` or `CY`. The requirement that it be the *same period* weighs against guessing, but does not fully spell out those boundaries.\n\n2. **“The year may be written anywhere in the item for the same period …” — Clear in the illustrated case; ambiguous at the boundary.** If the article says “the second quarter of fiscal 2027” and later refers to **that quarter**, `FY2027-Q2` is supported. If a year appears elsewhere but its connection to the figure’s quarter is only inferred, “for the same period” leaves the strength of that connection open.\n\n3. **“null when no period is given, when the item never writes that year … or when the period is not one year or one quarter …” — Clear for the listed cases.** A quarter or “the full year” with no written year takes `null`. Another fiscal year that would require calculating the figure’s year also takes `null`. A range or deadline takes `null`. The original stated wording can still be preserved in the separate `period` field.\n\n4. **“Never work the year out from the publication date or from knowledge outside the item.” — Clear as to the year.** Unlike c21, it no longer expressly prohibits converting a calendar period into a fiscal period. “The same period” suggests preserving the original basis, but the prohibition now applies specifically to working out the **year**, leaving a possible conversion of quarter or FY/CY basis less explicit.\n\nThe requested examples therefore read as follows:\n\n| Article wording | What c22 says |\n|---|---|\n| A quarter, with its fiscal year written elsewhere | Use the fixed form **if the article connects that written year to this same quarter**. A mere nearby year is not enough by the stated rule; borderline connections remain ambiguous. |\n| A quarter, with only another fiscal year written | `null` if the quarter’s year must be worked out from that other year. |\n| “The full year” | `null` if its year is not written; otherwise a fixed year only when that year is for this period. |\n| “Next fiscal year” | `null` if its year is not written; use the fixed form if the article explicitly identifies that same year. |\n| A calendar-year figure | `CY` form when the article states the year and establishes it as a calendar period. A bare year’s FY/CY interpretation is not explicit. |\n\n### 2. Possible effects on the answer\n\nThese are **wording risks, not observed model failures**:\n\n- **A stated source could be left empty.** “The item states in its own voice without saying whose it is” could be applied to a sentence that omits an attribution even though another part of the article explicitly connects the figure to management or an analyst.\n- **A figure could be omitted.** The `party` field is required, but c22 removed c21’s general fallback, **“null when the item does not say whose figure it is.”** It supplies `null` explicitly only for unattributed forecasts. A reported or other figure with an unclear source has no equally explicit fallback, although the schema allows `null`. Omitting it would conflict with the instruction to list all useful figures.\n- **Splitting is not instructed.** The new wording expressly says the statement stays one item, consistent with the existing array description and closing check. I see no changed words that direct a split; model variation remains possible.\n- **Keeping, opening, and linking are not directly changed by either field description.** The classification conflict could nonetheless influence whether the model treats an unattributed forecast as a qualifying figure: section 2 describes figures as a reported result, guidance, or **“a named party’s or consensus estimate.”** Similarly, an incorrectly inferred or emptied period could influence a link, despite the instruction that **“a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked.”** There is no new instruction to open a question.\n\n### 3. Smallest wording changes\n\nThese are proposed wording only; no file was changed.\n\n- In `party`, qualify the unattributed-forecast sentence: **“A forecast for which the item does not state a source, including elsewhere in the item, has no stated source …”** This preserves `null` for a genuinely unattributed forecast while allowing an explicit cross-reference to an earlier source.\n- Restore the general fallback: **“`null` when the item does not state the figure’s source.”**\n- In `kind`, make its estimate definition agree with the existing new instruction: **“estimate: a forecast other than the company’s stated outlook, including a named party’s or consensus forecast and an unattributed forecast.”** This resolves the contradiction without adding a keep, open, or link rule.\n- In `fiscal_period`, add: **“For a quarter, the item must also state its quarter number and identify it as part of that year; use the fiscal or calendar basis the item states.”** Restore c21’s **“do not convert a calendar period into a fiscal one.”** This makes the fixed form’s supporting information explicit without calculating a missing year."}}
{"type":"turn.completed","usage":{"input_tokens":200369,"cached_input_tokens":163072,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3098,"reasoning_output_tokens":25}}
